---
import Docs from '../../layouts/Docs.astro'
import Callout from 'web-design-system/astro/marketing/Callout.astro'
import CodeBlock from 'web-design-system/astro/product/CodeBlock.astro'
import TableScroll from '../../components/TableScroll.astro'
import { REPO_URL } from '../../data/docs'

const toc = [
  { label: 'What you need', href: '#what-you-need' },
  { label: 'Run it from a checkout', href: '#from-a-checkout' },
  { label: 'Build a macOS app', href: '#macos-app' },
  { label: 'First launch', href: '#first-launch' },
]

const clone = `git clone ${REPO_URL}.git
cd todo-lofi

# Run the desktop app
cargo run -p todo-2`

const requirements = [
  { need: 'macOS', detail: 'macOS 12 or newer. The bundle sets that as its minimum system version.' },
  { need: 'Linux', detail: 'The Rust crates build on Linux; GPUI needs the platform graphics and input libraries.' },
  { need: 'Rust', detail: 'A toolchain new enough for edition 2024 (1.85+), plus the platform GUI dependencies.' },
  { need: 'Nix (optional)', detail: 'The dev shell provides the pinned toolchain, cargo-watch, Node, and pnpm — see Development setup.' },
]
---

<Docs
  title="Install and run"
  description="todo-lofi is a desktop app for macOS and Linux. There are no installers yet, so today you run it from a checkout of the repository."
  toc={toc}
>
  <Callout tone="warning" title="Pre-release">
    There is no published download. The app is developed and run from the repository; everything below
    assumes you can clone it.
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="what-you-need">What you need</h2>
  <TableScroll label="Requirements">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>You need</th>
          <th>Details</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          requirements.map((requirement) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{requirement.need}</td>
              <td>{requirement.detail}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </TableScroll>
  <p>
    The least fiddly path is the development shell, because it brings the toolchain and every helper
    command with it. <a href="/docs/contributor/development">Development setup</a> walks through installing Nix and direnv;
    if you already have a workable Rust toolchain, you can skip it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="from-a-checkout">Run it from a checkout</h2>
  <CodeBlock filename="terminal" code={clone} showLineNumbers={false} />
  <p>
    The app starts with an empty database. Nothing leaves your machine: there is no account to create and
    no telemetry, and the network features stay off until you configure them.
  </p>

  <h2 id="macos-app">Build a macOS app</h2>
  <p>
    On macOS, the dev shell's <code>t2-bundle</code> assembles a real <code>todo-lofi.app</code> for you —
    it builds the binary, copies the icon and <code>Info.plist</code> into the bundle, signs it with the
    <code>todo-lofi-dev</code> identity when that certificate exists, and clears the quarantine flag. It
    assembles the bundle without launching it.
  </p>
  <p>
    That is also how release builds are produced, so it is the closest thing to an install today. For
    everyday development, run the raw binary instead (<code>t2</code>, or{' '}
    <code>cargo run -p todo-2</code>) — see{' '}
    <a href="/docs/contributor/development#command-reference">the command reference</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="first-launch">First launch</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><a href="/docs/tasks">Tasks, tags, and projects</a> — the four pages that follow cover the app itself, from the task list to the agent pane.</li>
    <li><a href="/docs/configuration">Configuration</a> — the in-app settings panes, plus model providers, Todoist, and GitHub credentials.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    A task list works offline out of the box; sync and the agent are the parts that need credentials.
  </p>
</Docs>
